=  Developing Custom Processes for IT Projects 
== Developing IT Project Management Methodology

Some organizations spend time and money training project managers in general methods, but trained managers may not know how to apply those methods to specific organizational needs. Therefore, organizations often develop their own IT project management methodologies using standard phases: Initiation, Planning, Executing/Production, Monitoring and Control, and Closing.

=== PRINCE2
- *PRINCE2* stands for *Projects IN Controlled Environments*.
- It is a structured project management method originally developed for IT projects, providing a step-by-step framework.

==== Eight Processes of PRINCE2
+ *Starting Up a Project:* Check whether the project should be initiated.
+ *Planning:* Prepare the detailed project plan.
+ *Initiating a Project:* Define the project scope and business case in detail.
+ *Directing a Project:* Provide overall executive direction to the project.
+ *Controlling a Stage:* Monitor and control each individual stage.
+ *Managing Product Delivery:* Ensure that required deliverables/products are produced.
+ *Managing Stage Boundaries:* Review one stage before authorizing the next.
+ *Closing a Project:* Complete and formally close the project.

=== Rational Unified Process (RUP) Framework
- *RUP* is an iterative software development process that builds software through repeated cycles.
- It supports object-oriented software development and is also known as the Unified Process (UP) Model.

==== Reasons for Using RUP
+ *Iterative:* Software is developed and refined through repeated cycles.
+ *Structured:* Provides a clear method for Object-Oriented Analysis and Design (OOA/D).
+ *Flexible:* Adaptable to project-specific requirements.

==== Phases of RUP
+ *Inception:* Collect requirements, determine scope and feasibility, and identify actors/users.
+ *Elaboration:* Prepare project plans, analyze risks, define non-functional requirements, design architecture, and complete use-case models.
+ *Construction:* Develop, integrate, and test software components, perform refactoring, and build a stable product.
+ *Transition:* Deliver software to users, complete deployment, and release the final product.

=== Six Sigma Methodology
- A method used to improve quality and eliminate defects by using data and statistical techniques.
- *DMAIC:* Used to improve an *existing* process.
- *DMADV:* Used to develop a *new* product or process.

==== DMAIC Process
+ *Define:* Identify the business problem, goals, scope, and resources.
+ *Measure:* Determine what to measure and collect accurate data.
+ *Analyze:* Examine data to identify root causes of problems.
+ *Improve:* Develop and implement solutions to remove root causes.
+ *Control:* Monitor the improved process to ensure lasting results.

==== DMADV Process
+ *Define:* Define project goals and design requirements.
+ *Measure:* Identify and measure quality requirements and risks.
+ *Analyze:* Evaluate alternatives and select the best design.
+ *Design:* Build and test the new product/process design.
+ *Verify:* Ensure the final product meets requirements in the real environment.

=== Customized SDLC & Process Tailoring
- *Customized SDLC (Multi-Pass Model):* Combines iterative, incremental, and Agile approaches through phases (Initial Requirements #sym.arrow Initial Solution #sym.arrow Customer Involvement #sym.arrow Final Solution).
- *Tailoring:* Adjusting project activities to match unique project characteristics while maintaining core goals.

==== Barriers to Tailored Project Management
+ Lack of top management support
+ Insufficient training
+ Lack of planning and resources
+ Poor sharing of lessons learned
+ "Not Invented Here" syndrome
+ Poor incentives and rewards
+ Excessive reviews

* Why Custom Processes Are Needed for IT Projects?*

- Customer role is vital
- Rapidly changing IT environments
- High complexity and uniqueness
- Elevated customer expectations for quality and service
- Shifting government involvement
- Diverse knowledge and expertise sources

== Preventive Measures for Office Computer Systems

Preventive measures are routine activities performed to protect hardware/software and maintain performance.

+ *General Issues*
    - Maintain proper environmental conditions (temperature, humidity, airflow).
    - Use stable, conditioned power supplies.
    - Keep computers away from strong magnetic fields.
    - Follow proper shutdown procedures.

+ *Daily Issues*
    - Run automatic virus scans.
    - Take differential or incremental backups of important data.

+ *Weekly Issues*
    - Delete temporary files and empty Recycle Bin.
    - Update antivirus software definitions.
    - Defragment hard disks when appropriate.
    - Clean printers.

+ *Monthly Issues*
    - Clean computer chassis, keyboard, mouse, and external drives.

+ *Yearly Issues*
    - Clean internal components and remove accumulated dust.

== Project Management Professional Certifications (PMI)

- *CAPM (Certified Associate in Project Management):* Entry-level certification for those with less experience.
- *PMP (Project Management Professional):* Advanced certification requiring proven project experience, adherence to a code of ethics, and passing an exam.

=== Major PMI Certifications
+ CAPM (Certified Associate in Project Management)
+ PMI-ACP (PMI Agile Certified Practitioner)
+ PMI-RMP (PMI Risk Management Professional)
+ PMI-SP (PMI Scheduling Professional)
+ PfMP (Portfolio Management Professional)
+ PgMP (Program Management Professional)
+ PMP (Project Management Professional)
+ PMI-PBA (PMI Professional in Business Analysis)

== Project Management Maturity (PMMM)

Project Management Maturity measures how well an organization develops, standardizes, and continuously improves its project management processes.

#figure(image("../../assets/image-38.png"),caption:"Project Management Maturity Model (PMMM)")
=== Five Maturity Levels
+ *Level 1: Initial* -- Processes are ad-hoc, informal, and chaotic; projects frequently exceed budget and schedule.
+ *Level 2: Managed (Repeatable)* -- Basic requirements and processes are planned, performed, measured, and controlled.
+ *Level 3: Defined* -- Standard organizational processes, procedures, and tools are documented and followed.
+ *Level 4: Quantitatively Managed* -- Processes are controlled using statistical and numerical data.
+ *Level 5: Optimizing* -- The organization focuses on continuous process improvement using feedback and performance metrics.

== Future Trends in Project Management
+ *Leadership and Communication Skills:* Greater emphasis on interpersonal effectiveness.
+ *Talent Retention:* Focus on attracting and retaining skilled professionals.
+ *Agile Framework:* Increased adoption to handle shifting requirements.
+ *Increasing Project Complexity:* Driven by rapid technological advancements.
+ *Remote Project Work:* Greater reliance on distributed and remote teams.

== Code of Ethics

A Code of Ethics provides guidelines for proper, responsible, and ethical decision-making in personal and professional contexts.

=== Main Principles
+ *Responsibility:* Being accountable to the community and society.
+ *Respect:* Maintaining professional respect for employers, clients, and team members.
+ *Fairness:* Treating people objectively without discrimination or bias.
+ *Honesty:* Being truthful in all personal and professional dealings.

* How to Create a Code of Ethics?*
+ Define the purpose of the code.
+ Write an introduction explaining expected benefits.
+ Define ethical rules regarding relationships, behavior, customers, and industry standards.
+ Determine implementation, monitoring, and enforcement mechanisms.

== Project Excellence, People Management, and Empowerment

Exquisite projects respect people, recognize skills, foster development, and build motivating environments.

=== Dealing Responsibly with People
- Match people's skills to project requirements.
- Maintain systematic and transparent communication.
- Respect moral and legal obligations concerning health and workplace safety.

=== Enablement vs. Empowerment
- *Enablement:* Helping people develop their knowledge, skills, personal interests, and career capabilities.
- *Empowerment:* Creating an environment where people are motivated to use their talents, take initiative, make decisions, and exercise confidence and authority.
